### Method reminder

An ADT is specified by its operations and their preconditions, never by its storage. State what each operation does to the data and when it may be used.

### Solution

**(a)** `push(x)` adds x on top; `pop()` removes and returns the top item; `peek()` returns the top item without removing it; `is_empty()` returns True when the stack holds no item. (An `is_full()` test for an array implementation is also accepted.)

**(b)** Precondition: the stack must not be empty. If it is, the operation must report an error (raise an exception or return an error code) and leave the stack unchanged, rather than return a meaningless value.

**(c)** The ADT is used only through its operations, which behave identically for any implementation; the storage is hidden (information hiding), so it can be changed without altering the calling programs.

A sample implementation, executed to check its behaviour (it prints `7 3 False`):

```python
class Stack:
    def __init__(self):
        self._items = []
    def push(self, x):
        self._items.append(x)
    def pop(self):
        if self.is_empty():
            raise IndexError("pop from empty stack")
        return self._items.pop()
    def peek(self):
        return self._items[-1]
    def is_empty(self):
        return len(self._items) == 0
s = Stack()
s.push(3); s.push(7)
print(s.pop(), s.peek(), s.is_empty())
```
